[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering the JUnit ExecutionCondition API for Custom Tagging and Disabling"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"JUnit 5 provides a powerful extension model that allows developers to customize test execution flow dynamically. One of the most effective tools for this is the ExecutionCondition API. By implementing this API, you can create custom annotations to enable or disable tests based on environmental variables, operating systems, custom properties, or business logic."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the ExecutionCondition Interface"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The ExecutionCondition interface resides in the org.junit.jupiter.api.extension package. It defines a single method: evaluateExecutionCondition(ExtensionContext context). This method returns a ConditionEvaluationResult, which dictates whether the container or test should be executed and provides a reason for the decision."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Step-by-Step Implementation of a Custom Condition"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a custom disabling mechanism, follow these two main steps: first, define a custom annotation, and second, implement the ExecutionCondition logic to interpret that annotation."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 1: Create a custom annotation annotated with @ExtendWith."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 2: Implement the ExecutionCondition interface to read the annotation values."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"@Target({ElementType.TYPE, ElementType.METHOD})
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@ExtendWith(EnvironmentCondition.class)
public @-interface RunOnEnvironment {
    String value();
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, write the corresponding condition evaluator logic that checks the system properties against the value supplied in the custom annotation."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"public class EnvironmentCondition implements ExecutionCondition {
    @Override
    public ConditionEvaluationResult evaluateExecutionCondition(ExtensionContext context) {
        Optional<RunOnEnvironment> optional = AnnotationSupport.findAnnotation(
            context.getElement(), RunOnEnvironment.class);

        if (optional.isPresent()) {
            String requiredEnv = optional.get().value();
            String currentEnv = System.getProperty("ENV", "dev");

            if (currentEnv.equalsIgnoreCase(requiredEnv)) {
                return ConditionEvaluationResult.enabled("Environment matches: " + currentEnv);
            }
            return ConditionEvaluationResult.disabled("Environment mismatch. Required: " + requiredEnv);
        }
        return ConditionEvaluationResult.enabled("No environment restriction found.");
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Applying the Custom Tag and Condition"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now that the extension is fully implemented, you can use your custom @RunOnEnvironment annotation on any test class or individual test method to control precisely when it should run."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"public class FeatureTests {

    @Test
    @RunOnEnvironment("staging")
    void testStagingFeature() {
        // This test will only execute if -DENV=staging is passed
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep condition evaluation fast and lightweight, as JUnit evaluates these checks frequently during the test discovery and execution phases."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always provide clear, descriptive reasons when returning ConditionEvaluationResult.disabled() to aid in debugging skipped tests."}]
